31
00:00:57,860 --> 00:00:59,760
Developers might imagine

32
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:01,700
that users, while using the product, would say to others

33
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:02,980
that this product is amazing

34
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:04,500
and you should see how well it works

35
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:05,760
But in reality, it often plays out differently

36
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:10,140
He said you should come and take a look

37
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:11,340
After using this product

38
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:12,980
I feel absolutely great

39
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:14,980
So this is a shift in perspective

40
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:17,640
Another scene we think is real goes like this

41
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:18,100
A person

42
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:20,040
He saw a piece of software or an app

43
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:21,080
His eyes lit up

44
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:23,040
This is the most incredible product I've ever seen

45
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:24,920
Actually, the real scenario is like this.

46
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:26,820
This man is the user of the product.

47
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:28,520
The other person is very impressed by him.

48
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:29,979
Saying you're really amazing.

49
00:01:29,979 --> 00:01:31,399
How did you manage to do that?

50
00:01:31,399 --> 00:01:32,219
In this situation,

51
00:01:32,219 --> 00:01:33,579
the man who uses the product,

52
00:01:33,579 --> 00:01:37,810
This guy just recommended the product he's been using.

53
00:01:37,810 --> 00:01:42,050
So, there's a rather perplexing Venn diagram here.

54
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:44,820
The goal of developers and development teams

55
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:47,180
is to have users think the product is great

56
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:49,320
but in reality, the user's goal is

57
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:51,100
that after using the product

58
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:53,440
I feel incredibly powerful

59
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:54,600
in short, I feel great

60
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:56,940
some might say these are the same thing

61
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:58,360
when I feel really capable

62
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:00,840
I also think the product developed by the developers is fantastic

63
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:03,360
When developers think their product is great

64
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:05,400
users will feel they are pretty capable

65
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:06,900
In fact, these are two different perspectives

66
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:07,920
At the root of it

67
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:10,380
people tend to focus on themselves

68
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:11,720
when they are using the software

69
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:14,780
they aren’t particularly concerned about who you are

70
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:15,980
what you have developed

71
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:17,260
who created the software

72
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:20,380
They only care about how it feels when they use it

73
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:21,740
and whether using this software

74
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:24,260
can help them become a better version of themselves

75
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:26,540
This is a relatively novel perspective in the book

76
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:27,540
I gave it a title

77
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:30,260
called 'Users Liking the Product and Liking Themselves'

78
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:31,800
Next, with the software in place

79
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:32,700
and users on board

80
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:35,940
you might start thinking about how to spread it

81
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:37,560
How to increase the user base

82
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:39,420
At this point, your first reaction might be

83
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,000
What can I do for myself

84
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:42,300
To promote myself

85
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:43,800
How to publish in various places

86
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:44,560
In this book

87
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:46,980
Leverage the user's points of interest from earlier

88
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:49,790
Usually, a conclusion about oneself

89
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:51,890
A better way to spread the word was introduced

90
00:02:51,890 --> 00:02:53,930
It leverages superior communication

91
00:02:53,930 --> 00:02:56,810
Here, users are categorized into two types

92
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:59,590
One type is the users with a fixed mindset

93
00:02:59,590 --> 00:03:01,650
The other type is the users with a growth mindset

94
00:03:01,650 --> 00:03:03,850
For users with a growth mindset

95
00:03:03,850 --> 00:03:06,370
Their learning capabilities tend to be stronger

96
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:09,010
Typically, when they use software

97
00:03:09,010 --> 00:03:11,570
They can tap into their creativity

98
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:16,390
Thus, they create products that are quite impressive

99
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:18,300
or some works of derivative creation

100
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:21,420
and then people will start to envy them

101
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:23,240
asking how they achieved success

102
00:03:23,240 --> 00:03:26,070
such users are referred to as outstanding users in the book

103
00:03:26,070 --> 00:03:29,590
and these outstanding users will become the envy of others

104
00:03:29,590 --> 00:03:32,290
people admire their output or their works

105
00:03:32,290 --> 00:03:33,710
when they seek advice from them

106
00:03:33,710 --> 00:03:36,230
they naturally mention your product

107
00:03:36,230 --> 00:03:38,110
so the book presents a viewpoint

108
00:03:38,110 --> 00:03:40,450
The best way to promote this product

109
00:03:40,450 --> 00:03:42,340
is to help a small group of users

110
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:44,090
become power users

111
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:44,990
In this way

112
00:03:44,990 --> 00:03:48,530
these power users will want their colleagues

113
00:03:48,530 --> 00:03:50,850
or friends to improve as well

114
00:03:50,850 --> 00:03:54,209
and use the same tools or products

115
00:03:54,209 --> 00:03:56,289
This is how your product spreads

116
00:03:56,289 --> 00:03:57,449
as mentioned in the book

117
00:03:57,449 --> 00:04:00,660
Users don't recommend products to friends because they like the product

118
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:02,760
but because they care about their friends

119
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:06,190
That's why he recommended this product to his friends

120
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:07,870
So we can think about it this way

121
00:04:07,870 --> 00:04:09,810
If a user

122
00:04:09,810 --> 00:04:11,820
or let's say this user is a student

123
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:13,460
the environment they're in

124
00:04:13,460 --> 00:04:16,220
is highly competitive

125
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:18,060
He dislikes everyone around him

126
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:20,820
So he encounters a product that makes him feel good about himself

127
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:22,260
a product that leaves him with a very positive impression

128
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:23,820
What would he definitely do?

129
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:26,260
He definitely won't recommend it to any of his classmates.

130
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:27,780
Because any of his classmates

131
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:30,160
in such a cutthroat competitive environment

132
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:32,980
would be perceived as a threat to him

133
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:34,480
So his thought is

134
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:37,730
I need to keep this product to myself and hide it

135
00:04:37,730 --> 00:04:38,730
I'll use it secretly

136
00:04:38,730 --> 00:04:39,650
Don't tell anyone

137
00:04:39,650 --> 00:04:41,330
So the spread of this product

138
00:04:41,330 --> 00:04:43,730
is also somewhat related to the environment

139
00:04:43,730 --> 00:04:44,920
This is one of my inferences.

140
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:47,120
The book also mentions a very interesting point

141
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:49,000
When you're developing software or a product

142
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
you need to design not only for your users

143
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,560
but also for your users' users

144
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:54,520
This user

145
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:57,790
Whenever I encounter this kind of suspicious nesting logic terms

146
00:04:57,790 --> 00:04:59,690
That immediately piqued my interest

147
00:04:59,690 --> 00:05:01,890
So I took some time to think about it

148
00:05:01,890 --> 00:05:03,610
And found something quite intriguing

149
00:05:03,610 --> 00:05:05,100
The user transmission chain of a product

150
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:06,800
Now, if you create a product

151
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,560
There will be all sorts of users

152
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:10,740
Users with different roles using your product

153
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:13,000
When they use your product,

154
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,530
they can create some by-products,

155
00:05:15,530 --> 00:05:18,560
or works created using your product.

156
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:20,720
Your product is typically a tool,

157
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:21,760
or something similar.

158
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:24,970
The by-products or works they create

159
00:05:24,970 --> 00:05:27,650
can be referred to as user-generated content,

160
00:05:27,650 --> 00:05:29,570
or user products.

161
00:05:29,570 --> 00:05:32,170
We are looking at this from the user's perspective.

162
00:05:32,170 --> 00:05:33,610
Let's look at the user again

163
00:05:33,610 --> 00:05:35,650
who generates secondary products

164
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:37,850
which in turn will have its own users

165
00:05:37,850 --> 00:05:41,210
This forms a tree-like, radiating model

166
00:05:41,210 --> 00:05:43,050
Now that we have users of users

167
00:05:43,050 --> 00:05:47,560
it's possible for the users of users to use the product

168
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:50,380
a tool generated by the higher-level user

169
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:53,900
to create outputs for their own users

170
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:56,360
and thus generate users of users of users

171
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:58,620
This can cascade down through multiple layers

172
00:05:58,620 --> 00:06:00,420
making it very complex and abstract

173
00:06:00,420 --> 00:06:01,560
Let's take a concrete example

174
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:05,290
Let's say there's a team working on an AI tool for enhancing productive force.

175
00:06:05,290 --> 00:06:08,870
Its users might be front-end developers

176
00:06:08,870 --> 00:06:09,870
or back-end developers

177
00:06:09,870 --> 00:06:10,910
or embedded system developers

178
00:06:10,910 --> 00:06:12,030
or game developers

179
00:06:12,030 --> 00:06:14,890
These developers are using productivity tools

180
00:06:14,890 --> 00:06:16,560
Improving your work efficiency

181
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:20,889
Front-end developers might use this AI-powered productivity tool to write code.

182
00:06:20,889 --> 00:06:21,529
and write it faster

183
00:06:21,529 --> 00:06:25,369
which could speed up the development of the website or product they're working on

184
00:06:25,369 --> 00:06:27,409
or accelerate the creation of desktop applications

185
00:06:27,409 --> 00:06:29,289
or speed up the development of mobile apps

186
00:06:29,289 --> 00:06:32,090
For front-end developers

187
00:06:32,090 --> 00:06:36,610
the audience and users of their websites might be math teachers

188
00:06:36,610 --> 00:06:42,260
For instance, if their website is for writing and recognizing LaTeX

189
00:06:42,260 --> 00:06:43,440
a website for math formulas

190
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:44,730
a small tool website

191
00:06:44,730 --> 00:06:47,050
so his users definitely include math teachers

192
00:06:47,050 --> 00:06:49,870
math teachers often use his website to write math formulas

193
00:06:49,870 --> 00:06:50,970
and prepare lesson plans

194
00:06:50,970 --> 00:06:52,810
for the math teacher

195
00:06:52,810 --> 00:06:54,330
he definitely has his students

196
00:06:54,330 --> 00:06:58,480
his students are a large group of math majors

197
00:06:58,480 --> 00:07:00,960
what's the use of understanding this radiating model?

198
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:04,840
By thinking step by step and looking ahead

199
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:08,580
you can identify potential secondary user groups

200
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:11,700
and the size of these secondary user groups could be substantial

201
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:13,680
Once you understand the secondary user groups

202
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:15,540
such as students in the mathematics department

203
00:07:15,540 --> 00:07:19,250
you might influence the direction of your product

204
00:07:19,250 --> 00:07:20,190
in terms of its orientation

205
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:21,670
you might start

206
00:07:21,670 --> 00:07:24,270
For your AI tool that boosts productive force

207
00:07:24,270 --> 00:07:28,050
You might want to increase the training on LUTEXT's grammar.

208
00:07:28,050 --> 00:07:28,950
After the training is completed,

209
00:07:28,950 --> 00:07:30,610
for a front-end developer,

210
00:07:30,610 --> 00:07:31,650
once they start building a website,

211
00:07:31,650 --> 00:07:34,550
when they get to the part of writing a small module for mathematical formulas,

212
00:07:34,550 --> 00:07:36,490
they will find that the website they can build

213
00:07:36,490 --> 00:07:38,650
seems to have better support for mathematical formulas.

214
00:07:38,650 --> 00:07:41,590
As a result, the math teacher also finds it more useful.

215
00:07:41,590 --> 00:07:43,770
They start using the website more smoothly.

216
00:07:43,770 --> 00:07:47,660
As a result, the students became even more satisfied with the teacher.

217
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:50,280
The math teacher also felt very satisfied.

218
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:52,909
He provided feedback to the front-end developer.

219
00:07:52,909 --> 00:07:55,829
Consequently, the front-end developer also felt very satisfied.

220
00:07:55,829 --> 00:07:58,329
He might even become exceptionally outstanding.

221
00:07:58,329 --> 00:07:59,189
In this situation,

222
00:07:59,189 --> 00:08:01,640
the front-end developer would become an excellent user.

223
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:05,520
As a result, he will be more willing to share AI productive force tools.

224
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:07,060
That's the logic, right.

225
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:08,580
So, this user

226
00:08:08,580 --> 00:08:10,480
has a very innovative mindset

227
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:11,620
As we mentioned earlier

228
00:08:11,620 --> 00:08:12,740
users are not fond of the product

229
00:08:12,740 --> 00:08:14,350
but they love themselves

230
00:08:14,350 --> 00:08:17,210
and their own excellence

231
00:08:17,210 --> 00:08:20,240
and the desire to show off, which drives them to spread it

232
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:21,240
which might be the most effective approach

233
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:22,120
though we shouldn’t call it showing off

234
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:24,620
It's about the psychology of wanting to help others

235
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:27,250
The book then mentions a new

236
00:08:27,250 --> 00:08:28,350
point worth pondering

237
00:08:28,350 --> 00:08:30,570
It's crucial to create an outstanding product

238
00:08:30,570 --> 00:08:34,500
but it's equally important that the users themselves are capable

239
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:35,659
This is a user-centric mindset

240
00:08:35,659 --> 00:08:37,860
So in the title, I wrote product

241
00:08:37,860 --> 00:08:39,568
outstanding vs. user capability

242
00:08:39,568 --> 00:08:42,369
Sometimes we might observe certain products

243
00:08:42,369 --> 00:08:44,569
It's a sustainable success.

244
00:08:44,569 --> 00:08:47,640
The book mentions this kind of sustainable success.

245
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:50,040
It's not crucial in the product itself.

246
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:53,240
But the key lies in their users.

247
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:57,190
So sometimes when we want to study this product,

248
00:08:57,190 --> 00:08:58,550
or similar products,

249
00:08:58,550 --> 00:08:59,530
why they succeed,

250
00:08:59,530 --> 00:09:00,940
we might think about

251
00:09:00,940 --> 00:09:02,260
breaking down its features.

252
00:09:02,260 --> 00:09:04,040
to analyze the functionality it provides

253
00:09:04,040 --> 00:09:06,020
and identify exactly what needs it addresses

254
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:10,300
This is a very objective and conventional approach

255
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:12,360
However, we might overlook one aspect

256
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:15,160
The users of this product are themselves very capable

257
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:16,060
Just like some schools

258
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:18,420
which cherry-pick their students

259
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:21,520
may not have the most outstanding educational resources

260
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:24,140
and the reason they excel is

261
00:09:24,140 --> 00:09:26,880
The students at these schools are already very capable.

262
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:28,180
So we can infer that

263
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:30,560
when you want to create a software or a product,

264
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:33,400
you need to clearly define your user base,

265
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:35,600
what your core user group looks like,

266
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:36,980
your secondary

267
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:40,470
and the next layer of user profiles look like,

268
00:09:40,470 --> 00:09:43,430
and what the outer layer looks like.

269
00:09:43,430 --> 00:09:46,230
If my user isn't as capable as you

270
00:09:46,230 --> 00:09:47,430
What should we do?

271
00:09:47,430 --> 00:09:49,870
The book even mentions a new approach

272
00:09:49,870 --> 00:09:51,210
called upgrading users

273
00:09:51,210 --> 00:09:53,630
The term 'upgrading users' might sound a bit off

274
00:09:53,630 --> 00:09:57,230
as if people are being objectified like parts

275
00:09:57,230 --> 00:09:58,470
and treated as something to be upgraded

276
00:09:58,470 --> 00:10:01,650
Actually, what it means is that we should empower our users

277
00:10:01,650 --> 00:10:03,850
